Euler's Totient Function (phi-function):
It count the number of integers between 1 and n inclusive, which are gcd(a[i], n) == 1

Properties:
- if p is prime, then phi(p) = p-1;

- phi(p**k) = p**k - p**(k-1) # if p is prime and k >= 1

- It's Multiplicative:
if a and b are relatively prime (coprime), then:
    phi(a*b) = phi(a) * phi(b)

- if a and b are not coprime, then:
    d = gcd(a, b)
    phi(a*b) = phi(a) * phi(b) * d/phi(d)

- phi(n) = n * (1-(1/p1)) * (1-(1/p2)) * .. * (1-(1/pk))

    where p1..pk are prime factors of n.

- sum of all phi(d[i]) = n ,  where d are the divisors of n


phi implementation using factorization on O(sqrt(n)):

int phi(int n) {
    int result = n;
    for (int i = 2; i * i <= n; i++) {
        if (n % i == 0) {
            while (n % i == 0)
                n /= i;
            result -= result / i;
        }
    }
    if (n > 1)
        result -= result / n;
    return result;
}

Euler torient from 1 to n in O(nloglogn):

void phi_1_to_n(int n) {
    vector<int> phi(n + 1);
    for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++)
        phi[i] = i;

    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
        if (phi[i] == i) {
            for (int j = i; j <= n; j += i)
                phi[j] -= phi[j] / i;
        }
    }
}


